WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Begin with the business. Ask what the company sells, who its customers are, and where its revenue comes from before looking at the share price.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Look at financial reports. Public companies publish reports with financial results and risk discussions. Read them instead of relying on a headline.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Revenue and profit. Revenue is money earned from sales. Profit remains after expenses. Growing sales do not guarantee growing profit.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Debt matters. Borrowing may help a company operate or expand, but debt also creates obligations. Look at the business in context.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Read the risk section. Competition, costs, regulation, and other events can change a company’s outlook. Risks differ by business.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Compare over time. One quarter may be unusual. Comparing several periods can reveal changes, though past performance cannot predict the future.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Question the source. A confident stock tip is not evidence. Check the source and whether the person may benefit from your decision.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Write a short summary. In your own words, explain what the company does, one strength, one risk, and what information you still need.
